## [v2.2.1] - 2026-09-22

### Security
- **UDP multicast source filter validated at config save (T1.2)** — the `source_filter` field on the UDP multicast inbound listener was applied at socket-open time; an invalid entry (bad IP, CIDR, or hostname) crashed the join with an obscure `inet_aton` error at listener startup. `validate_config()` now rejects invalid `source_filter` entries and non-multicast `multicast_group` addresses at save time, so operators see the mistake immediately in the UI. The IGMPv3 source-specific multicast join (`IP_ADD_SOURCE_MEMBERSHIP`) was already wired; help text now documents that the kernel drops unauthorised senders before userspace sees the packet, blocking spoofed CoT injection from other hosts on the same group
- **Inbound `auth_mode=none` requires an ip_allowlist (T1.3)** — the generic JSON and XML inbound plugins accept `auth_mode: none` for testing, which leaves `POST /api/inbound/<id>/data` world-accessible. Unauthenticated attackers could inject arbitrary CoT into every attached TAK server. `validate_config()` now raises with an operator-readable message when `auth_mode=none` has no `ip_allowlist`; `0.0.0.0/0` is accepted as an explicit wildcard so the choice is auditable rather than implicit. `_check_ip_allowlist()` at `routes/inbound.py:73` widened to accept both the legacy JSON-array format (backward compatible) and newline/comma-separated text. Save-time validation wired into `update_stream_safely()` so the error surfaces in the UI, not just at listener startup
- **LDAP StartTLS/LDAPS enforced on every bind (T4.1)** — prior behaviour bound users and the service account with `Connection(..., auto_bind=False)` and never issued `start_tls()`. `use_tls: true` was a silent no-op — every credential went to port 389 in cleartext, and `validate_cert: true` provided false assurance because no TLS handshake ever occurred. `_get_connection()` now calls `connection.start_tls()` between construction and the caller's `.bind()` when `use_tls=true` and `use_ssl=false`; a failed upgrade raises `ProviderConnectionException` before any bind is attempted. `validate_configuration()` refuses plaintext-and-`validate_cert=false` configs unless the operator sets `allow_insecure=true`; when opted-in, every startup emits a WARNING naming the server and the specific insecure option
- **Failed-login lockout admin unlock action (T1.4)** — the failed-login counter, `is_locked()` check, and lock/unlock methods already existed and integrated with the local auth path; two gaps remained. `config/schema.py` declared `lockout_duration: int = 900` (15 min in seconds) while `services/auth/auth_manager.py` reads `lockout_duration_minutes` with a 30-min default — the dataclass field was documentation-only but misleading. Renamed to `lockout_duration_minutes: int = 30` with validator bounds of 1-1440 minutes so both artefacts agree with the runtime. Added `POST /auth/admin/users/<id>/unlock` route with an Unlock button on the admin user detail page that only renders when the user is both locked and still ACTIVE — clears the lockout without mutating status (previously admins had to click Enable, an accidental side-effect nobody should have to memorise)
- **Plugin signatures enforced on sideload (V1/V2)** — files placed in `external_plugins/` loaded at startup without any cryptographic check; an attacker with filesystem write got RCE at the next restart. The ZIP install path already verified signatures via `services/plugin_package_verifier.verify_package_signature()`; the sideload paths did not. `_load_package_plugins()` now gates on the existing package verifier and `_load_external_plugins_directory()` gates on a new `verify_single_file_signature()` for loose `.py + .py.sig` pairs. Signed plugins always load; unsigned community-tier plugins are refused unless the operator opts in via `plugin_settings.allow_community_sideload: true` in `config/settings/plugins.yaml`; unsigned pro/enterprise plugins are always refused regardless of the toggle
- **Trivy scan findings closed for pip / setuptools / msgpack** — image scan flagged 9 CVEs across three undeclared or unpinned packages. `Dockerfile:28` gains explicit floors on `pip>=26.2.0` and `setuptools>=83.0.0`; `pyproject.toml` and `requirements.txt` add `msgpack>=1.2.1` (transitive via aiocache) and tighten `setuptools>=83.0.0`; `[build-system].requires` also lifted to `setuptools>=83.0.0` so the PEP-517 bootstrap resolver picks the safe version. Floors are not equality pins — the resolver stays free to pick newer versions but nothing below the fixed threshold can resolve. Closes GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964, GHSA-4xh5-x5gv-qwph, GHSA-qwm4-qh6w-59xr, GHSA-58qw-9mgm-455v, GHSA-jp4c-xjxw-mgf9, GHSA-wf93-45jw-7689, GHSA-6vgw-5pg2-w6jp, CVE-2025-47273, CVE-2026-59890

### Added
- **`plugin_settings.allow_community_sideload` config key** in `config/settings/plugins.yaml` — opt-in for unsigned community-tier plugins in `external_plugins/`. Default false; every unsigned load produces a WARNING naming the file
- **New `verify_single_file_signature()` helper** in `services/plugin_package_verifier.py` — validates a `<name>.py + <name>.py.sig` pair using the same Ed25519 trust root and result contract (`verified` / `unsigned` / `invalid`) as the package verifier
- **New `ip_allowlist` textarea field** on both `generic_inbound_plugin` and `generic_xml_inbound_plugin` — accepts comma- or newline-separated IPs and CIDR blocks (including `0.0.0.0/0` as an explicit wildcard)
- **New `allow_insecure` config field** on the LDAP provider — deliberate escape hatch for plaintext LDAP or TLS without cert validation. Default false; opting in triggers a startup WARNING per-connection
- **Plugin Signing section added to `docs/PLUGIN_SECURITY.md`** — load-time behaviour matrix (ZIP install vs package sideload vs loose .py, signed vs unsigned, community vs pro/enterprise), the sideload toggle, and where signatures come from (TrakBridge Project offline key; signing tooling lives in `trakbridge-plugins-premium`)

### Internal
- 84 new regression tests across the 2.2.1 fixes:
  - `test_t1_2_udp_multicast_source_filter.py` — 12 tests: config field presence, SSM vs standard join selection, whitelist rejection paths
  - `test_t1_3_inbound_auth_mode_validation.py` — 28 tests: field presence, validation enforcement (both plugins parametrized), parser format acceptance (JSON legacy + newline/comma), runtime allowlist behaviour, fail-closed on malformed configs
  - `test_t1_4_login_lockout.py` — 14 tests: DB-backed integration tests for counter increment/reset, lockout after N failures, correct-password refusal while locked, admin unlock round-trip, CSRF required, template button visibility, schema alignment
  - `test_t1_4_t4_1_ldap_secure_defaults.py` — 15 tests: validator accept/reject across each config permutation, WARNING logs for opt-in vs absence for shipping default, `start_tls()` called once on StartTLS path and never on LDAPS or plaintext opt-in, start_tls failure raises and prevents bind, anonymous and user-cred paths both covered
  - `test_v1_v2_plugin_signature_verification.py` — 15 tests: new verifier helper, package sideload gate (signed/unsigned + community/pro), loose-py sideload gate, config reading, digest-contract check that locks the algorithm against silent drift with the external signer
- Pre-existing `tests/unit/test_plugin_manager_packages.py` fixture updated to opt in to community sideload (those tests target whitelist/tier semantics, not signatures); one pro-tier test patches the verifier for the same reason

### Upgrade notes
- **Straight upgrade from 2.2.0.** No schema migration, no new secrets required
- **LDAP configs with `use_tls: true` on port 389 will now actually negotiate TLS.** Previous behaviour silently sent credentials in cleartext despite `use_tls: true`. Deployments where the LDAP server does not support StartTLS on 389 will start failing binds — either configure the server to accept StartTLS, switch to LDAPS on 636, or (as an audited last resort) set `allow_insecure=true` in the provider config to preserve the old cleartext behaviour with a boot-time WARNING
- **Existing streams with `auth_mode=none` and no `ip_allowlist` will fail validation on next save.** The listener continues to run with its stored (pre-2.2.1) config until restart; on restart the invalid config is refused with the same error. Set an explicit `ip_allowlist` (use `0.0.0.0/0` to preserve the old world-accessible behaviour with an audit trail)
- **Loose `.py` files in `external_plugins/` will be skipped at startup unless signed or the operator opts in.** Signed packages continue to load unchanged. Set `plugin_settings.allow_community_sideload: true` in `config/settings/plugins.yaml` to preserve the pre-2.2.1 behaviour for community-tier plugins; pro/enterprise unsigned sideloads remain refused
- **Container image rebuild required to pick up the new pip/setuptools/msgpack floors.** Existing running containers keep their old versions; the fix applies at rebuild

---

## [v2.2.0] - 2026-09-06

### Security
- **Preview endpoints now require authentication** — `GET`/`DELETE /api/inbound/<id>/preview` and `POST /api/inbound/<id>/preview/remap` accepted anonymous calls, letting anyone with network reach read captured payloads (including headers and source IPs), wipe the capture buffer, or re-run the parser with an attacker-supplied `plugin_config`. All three endpoints now require a session cookie or `tb_pat_` bearer via `@api_key_or_auth_required`; the CSRF exemptions that were compensating for the missing auth gate are removed
- **Reconnaissance endpoints tightened** — `/api/status`, `/api/version`, `/api/health/ready`, and `/api/health/live` previously returned application state and version to any unauthenticated caller. Now gated behind `@api_key_or_auth_required`; `/api/health` remains the sole public probe for container/load-balancer checks. Orchestrators and monitors already carry a service credential
- **Outbound-plugin SSL fallback removed** — Garmin, Discord and Slack plugins caught `ssl.SSLError` and retried the same request with `ssl=False`, re-sending credentials or webhook URLs over an unverified channel. A MITM only needed to present an invalid cert to harvest them. The fallback blocks are deleted from both the message-send and test-connection paths; SSL errors now surface as ERROR log entries and the operator sees the real failure instead of a misleading "success (insecure)" result
- **Stream detail page no longer renders decrypted plugin config into JavaScript** — the Test Connection button on `/streams/<id>` iterated `stream.get_plugin_config()` (the *decrypting* accessor) and embedded each field into an inline `<script>` literal. Any user who could load the page — including a VIEWER — could Ctrl-U and read every credential: Garmin passwords, API keys, MQTT broker passwords, Discord/Slack webhook URLs. The button now calls `/streams/<id>/test` which loads the config server-side; no decrypted value crosses the wire back through the browser
- **TAK server delete guard extended to many-to-many links** — the previous guard only checked the legacy `Stream.tak_server_id` foreign key. The newer `stream_tak_servers` M2M association uses `ondelete=CASCADE`, so a stream linked only via the M2M table was silently orphaned when its server was deleted — the association row cascaded away and the stream kept reporting success while delivering to one fewer destination. Guard now checks both relationships; delete is refused if either references the server
- **Bearer-bypass CSRF shim compatible with Flask-WTF 1.1 and 1.2** — the shim wrapping `CSRFProtect.protect` had a fixed signature that only worked on one Flask-WTF version. 1.1's before-request hook calls `protect(apply_exemptions=True)`; 1.2 dropped the kwarg and calls `protect()`. Any container image on the wrong version 500'd every request before reaching auth. The shim now accepts `*args, **kwargs` and forwards through — it doesn't need to know Flask-WTF's own contract. Only surfaced once the T1.1 fix removed the CSRF exempts that had been masking the mismatch on 1.1 images

### Added
- **User API keys for scripted access** — personal access tokens (`tb_pat_...`) usable as `Authorization: Bearer` alongside session auth. New `UserApiKey` model with per-key scoped permissions (e.g. `streams:read`, `api:read`), HMAC-SHA256 token hashing mixed with a server-side pepper, last-used tracking, and expiry. Self-service management UI at `/auth/api-keys` (create, reveal-once, revoke); admin cross-user view at `/admin/api-keys` with grouped scope chips. Bearer resolution is wired into the shared auth decorators, so every session-or-key endpoint accepts either; foreign-prefix and malformed tokens are rejected without touching the resolver
- **`@session_only` guard** — admin, credential-mutation, logout, and key-management routes explicitly refuse bearer auth with a consistent 401, independent of token validity. A leaked API key cannot manage keys, change passwords, or reach the admin surface
- **OpenAPI 3.1 spec and Swagger UI** — machine-readable spec at `/api/openapi.json`, interactive docs at `/api/docs`. Streams, plugins, and inbound endpoints documented; `API_REFERENCE` rewritten from the spec. Both endpoints are auth-gated (session or bearer) to keep the API surface out of anonymous-scanner reconnaissance; the spec is generated once per app instance and cached
- **Bearer-token log redaction** — `tb_pat_` values are scrubbed from log output
- **Circuit breaker tuning knobs documented** — `performance.yaml` `circuit_breaker` block now documents `timeout`, `half_open_max_calls`, `success_threshold`, `health_check_interval`, and `health_check_timeout` (all optional, sane defaults)

### Fixed
- **Circuit breaker recovery after TAK server outages** — the headline fix. Previously a lost TAK connection opened the per-server breaker and it never recovered; streams stayed dead until an operator edited and re-saved them. Root cause was structural: `_transmit_batch` swallowed socket errors into a `False` return, so the TX loop never broke, the reconnect loop never ran, and the breaker's recovery probe wrote to the same dead socket forever. The breaker now gates **connection establishment only**; socket errors propagate so the worker abandons the dead socket and reconnects; the per-iteration `manual_reset()` that neutered the breaker is gone (operator resets in `stop_worker`/`start_worker` preserved)
- **Streams no longer permanently deactivate** — 5 consecutive poll errors used to set `is_active=False` with no auto-reactivation. Streams now retry forever with capped exponential backoff (5 minutes max), log at ERROR at the threshold and every 10th failure after, and surface the outage via the stream's `last_error` field. Recovery on feed/server restore is automatic
- **Feed-source outages no longer masquerade as healthy polls** — plugins report connection failures as `[{"_error": ...}]` sentinel dicts (a convention consumed by the connection-test UI); the poll loop counted them as retrieved locations, so a total feed outage showed a healthy stream with `last_error` clear. Sentinel-only results now raise and engage the retry/backoff/last_error machinery; mixed results (partial data) still count as success. Traccar's connection-error branches now return sentinels like its HTTP-status branches instead of an empty list indistinguishable from a quiet feed. Classified feed outages log as a single line, not a traceback
- **No more OPEN/HALF_OPEN flapping during outages** — the recovery timer used to admit real 30-second connection attempts as probes while the server was down. When a health check is registered (all TAK breakers), OPEN→HALF_OPEN is now driven solely by health-check success; breakers without health checks keep classic timer recovery. During an outage the breaker sits cleanly OPEN with fast-fail logs
- **Breaker no longer sticks in HALF_OPEN after recovery** — TAK breaker `success_threshold` default is now 1: one real established connection closes the breaker. Requiring a second success from health probes left it in HALF_OPEN indefinitely, because probes are unreliable alongside a live worker connection
- **Health probes no longer failed by TAK Server's close handshake** — probes connected instantly but hung in `wait_closed()`: TAK Server delays the TLS close for silent clients until its subscription reaper runs (~30s), past the probe timeout. Probe cleanup is now best-effort with a 2s cap and transport abort; the health verdict rests on the connect. Probe DB lookup moved off the event loop; probes slower than 5s log a per-phase timing breakdown (`db/config/connect/cleanup`); TAK probe timeout raised to 20s
- **Circuit breaker core hardening** — trips on *consecutive* failures (previously a lifetime cumulative count, so 3 failures spread over days tripped it); `half_open_calls` counts in-flight probes and is refunded on completion (concurrent healthy callers were starved during recovery); health checks probe in HALF_OPEN as well as OPEN, support sync callables, and log crashes at ERROR with the exception type (previously swallowed at DEBUG); healthy HALF_OPEN checks count toward `success_threshold`; config rejects `success_threshold > half_open_max_calls`
- **Parallel config systems collapsed into `ConfigLoader`** — `utils/config_manager.py` deleted (−366 lines); single loading path for all configuration
- **Backups directory created on first run** — setup no longer assumes it exists
- **Duplicate `monitoring:` key in `performance.yaml`** — the second block silently shadowed the first, discarding the fallback-statistics settings at load; merged into one block
- **Swagger UI external validator badge disabled** — no spec URL leakage to a third-party validator

### Changed
- **Operator model for degraded streams** — streams stay active through outages; watch **Last Error** on the stream (and `/api/health/circuit_breaker` for breaker state) rather than expecting streams to switch off. Events queued during a TAK outage drain on reconnect with newest-per-UID replacement; events in a batch that hits a dead socket are dropped by design (positions refresh next poll)
- **Worker log messages** drop the "Enhanced" prefix ("Transmission worker started"); breaker failure logs include the exception type when `str()` is empty (e.g. bare `TimeoutError`)

### Internal
- ~90 new tests across unit/integration/e2e, all written red-green, including a real-socket fake-TAK-server outage/recovery acceptance test (fails on pre-fix code) and a stream-resilience test asserting `is_active` survives 7 consecutive poll failures in the DB
- `reset_cot_service()` now clears `QueuedCOTService`'s class-level worker/connection dicts — stale entries from one test leaked into the next test's fresh service and triggered spurious dead-worker cleanup

### Upgrade notes
- **Schema migration required** (`add_user_api_keys`) — Docker deployments run it automatically via the entrypoint; source deployments require `flask db upgrade`
- **`API_KEY_PEPPER` secret required in production** — the app refuses to boot in production without it. Generate with `openssl rand -base64 48` and provide via Docker secret at `./secrets/api_key_pepper` (compose files already reference it) or the `API_KEY_PEPPER`/`API_KEY_PEPPER_FILE` environment variables. Non-production environments generate an *ephemeral* pepper per process with a startup WARNING — API keys created there are invalidated on every restart. Rotating the pepper invalidates every existing API key by design
- Streams no longer stop after repeated errors — remove any operational runbooks that instruct re-saving a stream to recover it; watch Last Error instead
- New `performance.yaml` `circuit_breaker` keys are optional; existing configs work unchanged. Note `failure_threshold` now genuinely counts *consecutive* failures, matching its long-standing comment
- Scripted API consumers can migrate from session-cookie auth to `Authorization: Bearer tb_pat_...` tokens created at `/auth/api-keys`; admin/credential routes intentionally refuse bearer auth

---

## [v2.1.1] - 2026-08-16

### Fixed
- **LDAP/OIDC users locked out of force-password-change flow** — `force_password_change_required` returned True for any user whose `password_changed_at` was NULL, but external-provider users legitimately have that column NULL (they have no TrakBridge-managed password to timestamp). Every LDAP/OIDC user was redirected to `/auth/force_password_change` on first login and could not complete the flow. The check now short-circuits `False` for any non-`AuthProvider.LOCAL` user; local users are unaffected
- **`SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE` env-var was a one-way switch** — the previous `_is_production or _secure_override in ("1","true","yes")` logic could only *force Secure on*; no value would turn it off in production. Operators running behind a non-TLS-terminating proxy (plain HTTP) had no supported escape hatch, and browsers silently drop cookies with the Secure flag on HTTP, breaking every session-authenticated request after login. The override is now bidirectional (`0`/`false`/`no` disables in production), and a WARNING is logged at startup when Secure is explicitly disabled in production
- **Duplicate startup log lines** — Flask's default handler on `app.logger` was emitting each record twice (once via that handler, once via propagation to the root logger); the default handler is now removed on startup so records propagate exactly once
- **Startup banner reported "unknown" for Python version, platform, and user** — the banner read `python_version`/`platform` off the top level of `get_version_info()`, but those fields live under the nested `environment` block; and `USER`/`USERNAME` env vars are typically unset in containers. Fields are now read from the correct nested block, and the current user is resolved via `getpass`/`pwd` fallbacks when the env vars are missing

### Changed
- **Plugin registry sync reconciles manifest drift** — `sync_plugin_registry` now detects when an on-disk `plugin.yaml` differs from the stored `InstalledPlugin` row and updates the row in place, writing a `PluginAuditLog` entry with action `metadata-synced`. Previously drift accumulated silently until reinstall. No-op when manifest, package_format, and is_verified all match, so audit churn stays proportional to real drift
- **Plugin version-compare exceptions no longer swallowed as "unparseable"** — `_version_satisfied` narrowed its blind `except Exception` to `(ValueError, AttributeError, TypeError)`. Real bugs (`ImportError`, etc.) now surface with a real traceback instead of being logged as parse failures

### Internal
- **CI setuptools-scm pretend version sourced from `pyproject.toml`** — dev/staging/main build jobs in `.gitlab/ci/build.yml` no longer hardcode `0.1.0.dev0`; the version being worked toward now lives at `[tool.trakbridge.release].next` in `pyproject.toml` and is read by CI. Tag builds continue to use `$CI_COMMIT_TAG`. Bumping the next-release target is now a one-line pyproject change reviewed alongside the work that motivates it, keeping plugin `min_trakbridge_version` gates honest on non-tag builds

### Upgrade notes
- Straight upgrade from 2.1.0. No schema migration, no configuration change required
- Operators running TrakBridge over plain HTTP in production (e.g. behind a non-TLS-terminating proxy) can now set `SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE=false` to make session cookies functional. This is not recommended — terminate TLS at a reverse proxy in front of TrakBridge for production deployments — and a WARNING is logged at startup when it's used
- Existing LDAP/OIDC users who were previously trapped in the force-password-change redirect can now log in normally. If you manually set `password_changed_at` in the database as a workaround, no action is required; the column is now ignored for external-provider users

---

## [v2.1.0] - 2026-08-09

### Security
- **CSRF protection enabled by default** — `WTF_CSRF_CHECK_DEFAULT` flipped to True; all session-authenticated POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE requests require a valid CSRF token. Five bearer/identity-authed endpoints (three inbound webhooks + two coordinate-conversion utilities) explicitly exempted with inline mechanism comments
- **Explicit session cookie flags** — `SESSION_COOKIE_HTTPONLY=True` and `SESSION_COOKIE_SAMESITE="Lax"` in every environment; `SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE=True` in production, env-var overridable elsewhere
- **Unauthenticated API surface trimmed** — eight previously `@optional_auth` health/monitoring/utility endpoints now require authentication; `GET /api/status` retained as the sole unauthenticated probe
- **CA cert upload filename sanitisation** — `_validate_and_read_ca_cert` applies `werkzeug.utils.secure_filename()`; empty results rejected via the existing error pattern
- **Master key value removed from DEBUG logging** — `services/encryption_service.py` no longer emits the generated master key. Rotation advisory: any master key generated while the deployment ran at LOG_LEVEL=DEBUG should be treated as compromised
- **Signed-plugin gate for premium tiers** — `install_plugin` refuses unsigned plugins whose manifest declares `tier: pro` or `tier: enterprise`, regardless of deployment tier. Unsigned community plugins continue to install everywhere with the UNVERIFIED warning
- **Plugin allow-list file hardening** — `update_whitelist_file` sets file mode `0o600` on the written allow-list and refuses to follow symlinks at the target path or its parent; `PermissionError` from either check is caught at the route layer and returned as `400`
- **Global fetch() CSRF interceptor** in `base.html` injects the token from a `<meta name="csrf-token">` tag on every same-origin fetch, covering both URL-string and `Request`-object call patterns
- **`CSRFError` handler** returns a clean 400 JSON/text response rather than a 500 stack trace

### Added
- **Plugin manager licence-tier badge** — the plugin manager page header displays the current deployment tier ("Licence: Community" / "Pro" / "Enterprise"); the upload modal body includes a hint noting which tier the deployment is licensed as

### Fixed
- **Route handlers catch `PermissionError`** — the plugin upload and lifecycle (`enable`/`disable`/`uninstall`) route handlers broadened their `except` clauses to `(PluginInstallError, PermissionError)` so symlinked whitelist paths return a controlled 400 instead of an unhandled 500
- **Multi-arch container images restored** — GHCR and Docker Hub release publish now preserves the multi-arch manifest end-to-end; arm64 was silently dropped from every public release since 2.0.0 by the previous pull/tag/push flow

### Internal
- Baseline test suite repaired (38 previously-failing tests fixed — stale test code from earlier refactors: singleton pattern, patch targets, threading-with-Flask-SQLAlchemy, isolation pollution)
- 40+ new tests across the security workstream covering CSRF flow, session cookies, endpoint auth, plugin tier UI, signed-plugin gate, whitelist file hardening
- Removed a stale `xfail` marker on `test_max_concurrent_tasks_limit` (feature works, marker converted a real pass to a silent XPASS)

### Upgrade notes
- Straight upgrade from 2.0.1. No schema migration, no configuration change required
- Any script or integration posting to session-authenticated endpoints with only a saved session cookie (no CSRF token) will now receive `400 Bad Request` — migrate to bearer/API-key auth or include `X-CSRFToken` header
- Existing user sessions keep their pre-2.1 cookies until expiry or logout; no forced re-login required

---

## [v2.0.1] - 2026-07-23

### Fixed
- **RX plugin dispatch on multi-server streams** — the RX-side plugin dispatch query in `services/cot_service_integration.py` filtered streams by the legacy single-server `tak_server_id` column, silently discarding events from every TAK server beyond the first for streams attached to multiple servers. Query now matches via either the legacy foreign key or membership in the `stream_tak_servers` junction table, mirroring the multi-first/legacy-fallback pattern used elsewhere
- **Hypercorn worker recycling removed** — `--max-requests 1000` was tearing down stream state and plugin caches every ~30 minutes on a healthy deployment. Removed from the Docker entrypoint. `HYPERCORN_MAX_REQUESTS` environment variable is no longer honoured (existing overrides in compose files are silently ignored)
- **Full COT service shutdown on process teardown** — `_cleanup_persistent_cot_service()` now awaits `QueuedCOTService.shutdown()` via `run_coroutine_threadsafe`, driving output plugin `cleanup()` hooks and flushing pending output-stat writes on SIGTERM. Buffering plugins (postgres archiver) no longer lose in-memory queues on graceful shutdown

### Changed
- Worker hygiene now relies on graceful shutdown and container health checks alone — Hypercorn worker behaves as a genuinely long-lived process

---

## [v2.0.0] - 2026-07-18

### Added
- **`trakbridge-plugin-sdk` on PyPI** — plugin base classes (`BaseGPSPlugin`, `BaseOutputPlugin`, `BaseInboundPlugin`) and their config/metadata infrastructure extracted from core into a standalone Apache-2.0 package. Third parties can `pip install trakbridge-plugin-sdk` and subclass a base class (a working plugin is ~30-40 lines). Runtime provider registry: core services (encryption, plugin metadata, circuit breaker, CoT delivery) injected via `trakbridge_sdk.configure(...)`. 17 built-in plugins now use the SDK through a re-export shim at `plugins/base_plugin.py`; no behaviour change for existing installs
- **Offline licence service** (`services/license_service.py`) — Ed25519-signed licence files verified against an embedded public key. Zero network calls, no phone-home. Three tiers: Community (default, no licence required), Pro, Enterprise. Licence file at `secrets/tb_license.json` by default, overridable via `TRAKBRIDGE_LICENSE_FILE`. Fails secure: missing, tampered, expired, or malformed licences degrade to Community with a logged warning. Live expiry re-checked on every query so a lapsing licence downgrades in place without restart. Admin about page shows tier badge, licence status, customer, expiry. In-app licence installation at `/admin/about` — signed files are verified before write; rejected uploads preserve current licence
- **Admin plugin manager** at `/admin/plugins` — replaces the "SSH in and drop a `.py` file" workflow. Plugins ship as `.zip` or `.tar.gz` archives with a `plugin.yaml` manifest, entry-point Python file, and supporting modules. 12-step install validation chain: archive safety (traversal, symlinks, decompression bomb caps), manifest structure, minimum TrakBridge version, tier gate, signature verification, AST code scan (rejects `exec`/`eval`/`os.system`/`subprocess`/`ctypes`), base-class inheritance verification, identity-rule enforcement. Rejected packages leave no trace on disk, in the database, or in the whitelist
- **Ed25519 signature verification** for plugin packages — signed Emfour packages show a "Verified" badge; tampered signatures are rejected outright; unsigned third-party packages install with an explicit "UNVERIFIED" warning
- **Plugin tier gating** — `plugin.yaml` gains an optional `tier` field enforced at both install time AND load time. A premium plugin cannot be loaded on a Community deployment even if manually copied into `external_plugins/`
- **Plugin lifecycle actions** — enable/disable toggles the whitelist without deleting files; uninstall removes files, database record, and whitelist entry. Both block cleanly if any active stream references the plugin
- **Plugin audit logging** on every action (install accepted/rejected, enable/disable/uninstall) with admin username, plugin id, tier, and verification status
- **New database tables** (Alembic migration `add_plugin_management_tables`): `installed_plugins` (id, version, tier, verified flag, enabled state, install metadata) and `plugin_audit_log` (action, admin username, licence id where applicable, outcome)
- **External plugin whitelist gating** — `external_plugins/<plugin_id>` files now require an explicit `external_plugins.<plugin_id>` entry in `plugins.yaml`, managed automatically by the plugin manager UI. Backwards compatible: startup auto-adds whitelist entries for any pre-existing external plugins

### Changed
- Plugin base classes moved from in-tree to `trakbridge-plugin-sdk`; existing plugins continue to work via the re-export shim
- External plugin loading is now whitelist-gated rather than allow-any-Python-file

### Fixed
- **`add_ca_cert_to_streams` migration** now uses `safe_add_column`/`safe_drop_column` and no longer crashes with `DuplicateColumn` on partially-populated Postgres schemas
- **Plugin category display** in the stream edit UI correctly reflects the CoT Forwarding vs Notifications split introduced in 1.3.0

### Upgrade notes
- Docker deployments run migrations automatically via `docker/entrypoint.sh`
- Source deployments require `flask db upgrade`
- Startup auto-adds whitelist entries for any pre-existing external plugins in `external_plugins/`; no manual whitelist edit required for the upgrade

